<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-normal-interpretation> {
  ex:l5-n10b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n10b-heading, ex:l5-n10b-plot, ex:l5-n10b-math ; cd:narration "The previous slide started with a concentration threshold and calculated its exceedance probability. Keep the same nitrate model and reverse that task: find the threshold with 95% of individual results below it. The standard-normal 0.95 quantile is about 1.64485362695. Multiply by SD 0.30 mg/L and add mean 10.0 mg/L to obtain 10.4934560881 mg/L, shown as about 10.49 mg/L. The theoretical CDF reaches 0.95 there, leaving 5% above. This is one-sided, not the interval between two symmetric bounds. The chart shows cumulative height, not area under a CDF. Connecting segments approximate the drawing; the quantile calculation uses the full distribution. Rounding to 10.49 gives about 94.88% below the rounded value rather than exactly 95%. The parameters remain specified, not estimated from the five illustrative values. Both calculations have now described one future measurement result. Before changing the setting, distinguish that target from estimating an unknown mean."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n10c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n10c-heading, ex:l5-n10c-list ; cd:narration "Return to the investigation: estimate the mean concentration in the defined water body at the stated survey time. The model quantile just calculated is a one-sided threshold for individual analytical results, not an estimate or interval for that environmental mean. Applying the same distance on both sides would contain only 90%, not a central 95%. A confidence procedure instead concerns a fixed unknown population mean and its coverage over repeated samples, provided its design and model assumptions hold. It does not contain 95% of individual values or assign a frequentist probability to the fixed mean after an interval is observed. The fixed-parameter quantile is also not a general prediction interval. Laboratory repeats of one collected sample cannot supply the missing environmental sampling variation. We therefore next compare within-sample analytical variation with variation between water units. That distinction determines which observations may support the environmental mean estimate and its uncertainty."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n11a-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:measurement-versus-site-variation ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n11a-heading, ex:l5-n11a-list ; cd:narration "N11a. The normal distribution is not universal. First identify what is being repeated or sampled. Technical repeats of one homogeneous nitrate sample under stable conditions involve analytical influences around one concentration plus bias. Across environmental sites, source strengths, transport, dilution, degradation, geology, sampling conditions and analytical error all contribute. These are different populations and variation mechanisms. A normal model for the first population cannot simply be transferred to the second. Neither population is guaranteed to follow any named distribution. The following two datasets are deliberately constructed teaching examples, not instrument or field measurements, and are not random simulations used to claim a fit."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n11b-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:measurement-versus-site-variation ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n11b-heading, ex:l5-n11b-text, ex:l5-n11b-plot ; cd:narration "The two settings need a visual comparison. Start with repeated measurements of one sample, the setting we already modelled. To make a histogram visible, use forty new deliberately constructed symmetric values, not extra observations added to the original five-value sample. Values at bin midpoints produce this illustration; no instrument observations or random generator are involved. Keep the specified normal reference, mean 10.0 mg/L and SD 0.30 mg/L, not a fitted line. Histogram heights are count divided by sample size and bin width, so areas sum to one and share density units L/mg with the curve. Every constructed value is counted once; curve coordinates are mathematical evaluations, not additional observations. Visual resemblance does not establish normality. Model tails extend beyond the plot and no formal fit test is performed. Next change only the kind of variation being illustrated: compare concentrations from different sites, where environmental processes add variability."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n11c-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:measurement-versus-site-variation ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n11c-heading, ex:l5-n11c-text, ex:l5-n11c-plot ; cd:narration "N11c. The forty deliberately constructed site concentrations are all positive and visibly right-skewed, with a long upper tail. They are not field data or draws from a declared lognormal generator. The normal reference matches their arithmetic mean 1.5125 mg/L and their divisor-n descriptive SD 1.33691576025 mg/L. Here divisor n means average squared deviation over all forty displayed values, not the sample variance with denominator thirty-nine. Matching two moments does not make the distribution normal or establish goodness of fit. The reference is symmetric, misses the shape and assigns about 12.8957% probability to negative concentrations. The histogram contains no negative observations; its empty negative bins expose this model mismatch. Density normalization allows bars and reference line to share a scale. The axes differ from the technical-repeat slide because the populations have different location and spread; compare mechanisms and shape, not raw bar heights between slides. Positivity alone is not proof of lognormality. Mixtures or censored measurements can also produce apparent skewness, and a normal analytical-error model can still be useful conditionally within a heterogeneous site population."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:l5-n11d-scene a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:measurement-versus-site-variation ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:l5-n11d-heading, ex:l5-n11d-list ; cd:narration "N11d. Check the process and reporting before selecting a family. Missing measurements and results below detection or quantification limits are not automatically true zero concentrations. Preserve censoring information rather than filling every such entry with zero. Investigate unusual values, sampling and laboratory provenance before excluding observations. Mixtures of populations, reporting limits and censoring can produce apparent skewness. Drift and correlated repeats violate the simple stable independent-repeat picture. Separate between-site variability from within-sample analytical variability: non-normal environmental concentrations can coexist with approximately normal analytical errors. Positivity and right skew make a lognormal model a candidate to examine, not an automatic conclusion. The next section introduces that model without claiming that these constructed site values follow it. Formal Kolmogorov-Smirnov and Pearson chi-square tests remain later work, not performed here."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
